RECOMMENDED FIX: Apply .translationTask as a conditional modifier at root level

The fix is to move .translationTask outside the .background block and apply it conditionally at the NavigationStack level. This preserves modifier identity across invalidate() calls.

Implementation strategy:
1. Create a custom ViewModifier that conditionally applies .translationTask on iOS 18+
2. Apply this modifier directly to NavigationStack (not nested in views)
3. This ensures the modifier maintains identity when invalidate() is called
4. The conditional availability check stays, but at modifier level instead of view creation level

Example structure:
```swift
struct ConditionalTranslationTaskModifier: ViewModifier {
    let configuration: TranslationConfigurationBox
    let action: (TranslationSession) async -> Void

    func body(content: Content) -> some View {
        if #available(iOS 18.0, macOS 15.0, *) {
            content.translationTask(configuration) { session in
                await action(session)
            }
        } else {
            content
        }
    }
}
```

Then apply as:
```swift
NavigationStack { ... }
    .toolbar { ... }
    .modifier(ConditionalTranslationTaskModifier(
        configuration: translationConfiguration,
        action: performTranslation
    ))
```

This approach:
- Maintains iOS 17 deployment target compatibility
- Preserves modifier identity across re-evaluations
- Allows invalidate() to work as designed
- Keeps the same code paths and logic

Wrap the symbol in a version check:

```swift
// In AboutView (MoreView.swift:495)
VStack(spacing: 12) {
    if #available(iOS 18, macOS 15, *) {
        Image(systemName: "character.book.closed.fill.zh")
            .font(.system(size: 80))
            .foregroundStyle(.tint)
    } else {
        Image(systemName: "book.closed.fill")
            .font(.system(size: 80))
            .foregroundStyle(.tint)
    }
    Text("SwiftMandarin")
        .font(.title)
        .fontWeight(.bold)
}
```

For the language picker in Settings, modify AppLanguage.iconName in LocalizationManager.swift:51:

```swift
var iconName: String {
    switch self {
    case .english: return "a.circle"
    case .chinese: 
        if #available(iOS 18, macOS 15, *) {
            return "character.book.closed.fill.zh"
        } else {
            return "book.closed.fill"
        }
    }
}
```

Alternative: use "book.closed.fill" unconditionally for both (simpler, works on all iOS versions, still represents a book/Chinese learning).

